## Objective

Close the production-blocking findings from the 2026-08-28 pre-demo audit in one reviewable hardening branch.

## Approved scope

- use a finite 18-hour workday access token and expire the web session on business-endpoint 401 responses;
- align Go BOM formula `B` with the plinth clearance contract;
- prevent quote acceptance when required plinth/material choices are unresolved and surface actionable errors;
- replace the Production dead-end with warehouse release guidance;
- make the demo cabinet seed resolve a non-empty BOM;
- reject empty or malformed project UUID fields with HTTP 400;
- serialize catalog saves and deduplicate identical toasts;
- mitigate stale cross-tab catalogs with BroadcastChannel plus refresh-on-return;
- add focused TS/Go/component tests and run repository gates;
- update `progress/audit-presentation-hardening-2026-08-28.md`.

## Session policy decision

The token TTL is 18 hours. Do not add proactive refresh from the former 15-minute plan: that would silently convert a finite workday session into an indefinitely renewable session. Immediate revocation remains server-authoritative because authenticated requests revalidate the user, membership and organization.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Exact audit regressions have executable coverage.
- [ ] `pnpm typecheck`, `pnpm test` and `go test ./...` pass, or any environment-only blocker is recorded precisely.
- [ ] Runtime repros are rechecked where the local demo environment is available.
- [ ] Cross-tab mitigation documents that per-entity optimistic concurrency remains a separate full solution.
- [ ] Demo cleanup is not executed without a backup and explicit confirmation immediately before destructive SQL.
- [ ] Branch uses conventional work-unit commits and opens a PR to `main`.

## Out of scope

- full per-entity catalog persistence and `If-Match` concurrency;
- Go synthesis of `ZOCLO-AUTO`;
- customer-picker search already tracked by #27;
- automated visual WebGL regression coverage.

## Problem

The Go BOM resolver now exposes module-level plinth clearance as formula variable `B`, but base treatment still differs from TypeScript outside that narrow contract.

Current gaps:

- Go does not synthesize the TypeScript `ZOCLO-AUTO` part;
- Go BOM does not filter ZOCLO components by effective base mode;
- project-item/layout base overrides are not carried into Go BOM resolution.

This can make quote calculation, layout and manufacturing output disagree for the same furniture item.

## Required outcome

Create one shared base-treatment contract consumed by TypeScript and Go that covers:

- `none`, `legs`, `plinth_board` and `plinth_strip`;
- module defaults and per-item/layout overrides;
- formula variable `B`;
- ZOCLO component inclusion/exclusion;
- automatic plinth-board synthesis with stable identity;
- negative cases proving no phantom plinth part appears.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] TS and Go resolve the same normalized BOM for every base mode.
- [ ] `ZOCLO-AUTO` semantics are explicit and tested in Go.
- [ ] Item/layout override wins over module default in both engines.
- [ ] Shared fixtures fail if either engine includes the wrong ZOCLO components.
- [ ] Existing plinth `B=100` audit regression remains green.

## Context

Follow-up from #441. This was intentionally outside the demo hardening slice because the exact audited `B` repro was already fixed and the broader contract needs its own review unit.

