## Objective

Measure and harden Granete for SketchUp for realistic large projects before introducing speculative optimizations, defining evidence-based budgets for insertion, rebuild, open/save, selection/inspector responsiveness, model size and generated ComponentDefinition lifecycle.

Tracks #465, #290 and #413.

## Mandatory authority

- `docs/architecture/sketchup-plugin-excellence.md`
- `docs/sketchup-excellence-execution-plan.md`
- `docs/architecture/sketchup-native-entity-model.md` + ADR-0004
- `docs/verification.md`

## Baseline architecture to preserve

V1 intentionally favors correctness:

- isolated top-level SketchUp ComponentDefinition per managed furniture instance;
- part definitions may be unique per occurrence;
- sharing is allowed only when definitions are immutable/content-addressed by a deterministic resolved geometry signature;
- no broad purge that can affect unrelated user content.

This issue must measure first and optimize only against demonstrated bottlenecks.

## Required benchmark fixtures

At minimum measure deterministic projects containing representative furniture/hardware/material complexity at:

- 10 furniture instances;
- 50;
- 100;
- 300;
- 500 when host/resources make the run practical.

Include repeated definitions and deliberately divergent instances.

## Metrics

Record at least:

- RBZ/plugin load time contribution where measurable;
- catalog/dialog usable time;
- insert latency;
- parameter/material rebuild latency;
- selection → inspector update latency;
- preflight result render/navigation latency after #466;
- open/save time;
- `.skp` size;
- process memory proxy where host tooling permits;
- number of generated furniture/part/hardware definitions;
- orphan-definition count after insert/update/delete/undo cycles.

Mark every metric `actual` with host/version/hardware context; no invented budgets.

## Optimization candidates only after baseline

Potential options include:

- immutable content-addressed part definitions keyed by `resolvedGeometrySignature`;
- lazy UI rendering/pagination/virtualization;
- scoped metadata indexes instead of repeated full scans;
- cache boundaries that remain tenant/session safe;
- asset deduplication.

No optimization may merge business identities or make shared mutable definitions.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Baseline report exists for 10/50/100/300 furniture fixtures on at least the primary supported host.
- [ ] Approved performance budgets are derived from measurements and documented.
- [ ] CI/nightly or reproducible host benchmark catches material regressions on the selected core fixture.
- [ ] Definition cleanup leaves no Granete-generated orphans after repeated update/delete/undo cycles.
- [ ] Any definition sharing introduced is immutable and cannot let FI-A rebuild mutate FI-B.
- [ ] Selection/inspector does not require an unbounded whole-model scan per event at scale.
- [ ] Performance optimization does not change manufacturing or business identity semantics.

## Negative proof required

Tests/review must reject:

- shared mutable top-level furniture definitions;
- geometry signature used as business identity;
- broad `purge_unused` or equivalent that affects unrelated model content;
- performance claims without host/version/fixture evidence;
- optimization that skips authoritative resolution to feel faster.

## Definition of Done

Granete has a reproducible large-project benchmark, measured budgets and implemented fixes for demonstrated P0/P1 bottlenecks while preserving identity isolation, host correctness and authoritative resolution.

## Objective

Turn the current macOS SketchUp 2026.2 evidence into an explicit commercial compatibility policy and real-host verification matrix across supported Windows/macOS SketchUp versions, Ruby/CEF combinations and RBZ/API compatibility.

Tracks #465, #290, #345 and #417.

## Mandatory authority

- `docs/architecture/sketchup-plugin-excellence.md`
- `docs/sketchup-excellence-execution-plan.md`
- `docs/verification.md`
- `apps/sketchup-extension/README.md`
- #355 packaging/update strategy

## Current evidence

#417 validated the native hierarchy and OpenCutList interoperability on real SketchUp 2026.2 macOS. Other versions/platforms must not inherit that support claim automatically.

## Required compatibility classification

For every matrix row use only:

- `supported` — mandatory host gate passes and release policy commits to support;
- `tested` — evidence passes but not necessarily a guaranteed support tier;
- `best_effort` — known usable but outside mandatory release gate;
- `unsupported` — blocked/known incompatible/not targeted.

Never use “should work” as support evidence.

## Matrix dimensions

Record at least:

- OS/version/architecture;
- SketchUp major/minor/build;
- embedded Ruby;
- CEF where relevant;
- Granete RBZ version/SHA-256;
- Granete API contract/version range;
- TestUp version;
- OpenCutList version when interop smoke applies.

Initial target matrix should cover commercially relevant SketchUp 2024/2025/2026 families on macOS and Windows, subject to actual available test hosts.

## Mandatory host gate suite

Each `supported` row must execute the applicable core host suite including:

- extension load/unload;
- HtmlDialog open/bridge;
- auth/session boundary smoke without exposing secrets;
- catalog fetch fixture/contract;
- native insert/rebuild/undo;
- selection/InstancePath behavior;
- material-aware rebuild;
- representative authoring/preflight UX as those issues land;
- save/reopen metadata roundtrip;
- legacy migration once #416 lands.

OpenCutList remains an optional/explicit interop row, not support authority for Granete.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A versioned compatibility matrix exists with evidence links per tested row.
- [ ] At least one Windows host row is executed before declaring Windows supported.
- [ ] Release notes and #355 consume the same support matrix.
- [ ] Unsupported/newer unknown SketchUp versions fail safely when contract/runtime incompatibility is known.
- [ ] A passing macOS row cannot automatically mark Windows supported.
- [ ] Host artifacts/logs are sanitized and record RBZ checksum.
- [ ] Required gate failure blocks promotion to `supported`.

## Negative proof required

- no support claim without real-host evidence;
- no simulated Ruby stubs counted as OS/SketchUp compatibility proof;
- no version mismatch may mutate model metadata before compatibility is established;
- diagnostics cannot contain credentials/private model paths by default.

## Definition of Done

Granete has an explicit, evidence-backed support matrix with at least the primary macOS and Windows targets, repeatable host gates and release policy integration so customers know exactly which SketchUp versions are supported.

## Objective

Define and implement safe degraded-mode behavior for Granete for SketchUp so temporary loss of connectivity, catalog availability or authoritative layout resolution never produces a managed object that appears manufacturable, publishable or release-ready based on local fallback geometry.

Tracks #465 and #290.

## Mandatory authority

- `docs/architecture/sketchup-plugin-excellence.md`
- `docs/architecture/sketchup-authoring-interaction-contract.md`
- `docs/sketchup-excellence-execution-plan.md`
- `docs/sketchup-manufacturing-contract.md`
- `docs/architecture/sketchup-interaction-model.md`
- #460 session/auth hardening
- #384 for publication/revision behavior

## Current concern

The extension has explicit static/development catalog and generic authoring fallback capabilities. Those are useful for bootstrap/tests and possibly preview, but commercial runtime must never blur the boundary between:

```text
resolved managed productive furniture
```

and:

```text
local/unresolved preview or authoring placeholder
```

## Canonical state model

Define explicit client-visible states such as domain-equivalent forms of:

- `resolved_current`;
- `resolved_stale`;
- `unresolved_preview`;
- `offline_cached`;
- `sync_required`;
- `blocked_incompatible`.

Do not overload a green/normal managed state.

## Allowed degraded behavior

Depending on state, the user may be allowed to:

- browse cached catalog metadata/thumbnails;
- inspect existing resolved furniture;
- move/rotate top-level furniture spatially;
- work on unmanaged room/decor geometry;
- prepare local authoring changes explicitly marked pending, if the contract supports safe reconciliation.

## Forbidden degraded claims/actions

Without authoritative current resolution:

- no manufacturing `ready`;
- no authoritative BOM/drilling/machining generation in Ruby;
- no DesignRevision publication as current truth unless #384 explicitly accepts the pending state;
- no ProductionRelease;
- no silent substitution of static/generic geometry as if server accepted it.

## Generic fallback policy

If generic/static geometry remains in production builds, it must be visually and semantically classified as non-productive preview and carry an explicit schema/state marker. Preferred option for connected production workflows is fail-closed rather than silently creating a normal managed furniture object.

## Reconnect/reconciliation

On reconnect:

- refresh session/catalog capability safely;
- detect stale catalog/definition/revision;
- resolve pending authoring intent through Granete;
- show conflicts before destructive replacement;
- preserve previous valid resolved state until new state is accepted;
- never create duplicate business identity as a retry side effect once #384 connected identity exists.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Network/layout failure cannot create a normal `manufacturable/ready` managed object from generic fallback geometry.
- [ ] Existing previously resolved furniture remains inspectable and is clearly marked stale/unavailable when appropriate.
- [ ] Offline/cache state is visible and distinct from license/auth failure.
- [ ] Publish/release paths fail closed when authoritative state is unavailable.
- [ ] Reconnect can refresh/resolve without deleting the previous valid hierarchy first.
- [ ] Pending local intent, if supported, is explicit and cannot masquerade as server-accepted truth.
- [ ] Static fallback used in tests/development is not silently active in connected production runtime.
- [ ] Tests cover network failure during insert, update, material change and reconnect.

## Negative proof required

- no local 18 mm generic box may be accepted as resolved manufacturing state merely because the server is unavailable;
- no offline action may generate server business IDs locally and later present them as authoritative;
- no network exception may downgrade `blocked/unavailable` to success;
- no stale cached catalog revision may overwrite newer accepted state silently.

## Definition of Done

The plugin has an explicit degraded-state contract and tests proving that connectivity loss may reduce convenience but never manufacturing integrity, identity integrity or publication safety.

## Objective

Provide one versioned, executable transport/resolve boundary for Granete for SketchUp to submit rich semantic furniture authoring intent beyond `widthMm/heightMm/depthMm` + material choices, so #467/#468 can move/add/remove internals and edit HardwarePlacement without inventing feature-specific query parameters, duplicating domain rules or creating persistent Project business state before Gate A.

Tracks #465 and #290.

## Hard prerequisites

- #346 semantic authoring metadata/round-trip baseline ✅
- #356 relationship/joint-driven machining baseline ✅
- #347 authoritative preflight ✅
- #415 native renderer ✅

Coordinates with #460 for the final SketchUp credential/capability model. This issue may define/implement the stateless resolve contract before full session hardening, but commercial use must not bypass #460.

## Mandatory authority

- `docs/architecture/sketchup-plugin-excellence.md`
- `docs/architecture/sketchup-authoring-interaction-contract.md`
- `docs/sketchup-excellence-execution-plan.md`
- `docs/sketchup-manufacturing-contract.md`
- #346 and #356

## Confirmed current transport limitation

The current layout boundary is optimized for the existing configurator and accepts dimension overrides plus board choices. Professional authoring requires richer intent such as:

- move a concrete internal component occurrence;
- add/duplicate/remove a component occurrence;
- change supported joinery intent;
- edit a manual HardwarePlacement;
- replace a hardware definition;
- resolve the full resulting layout/manufacturing feedback.

Do **not** extend the current query string indefinitely with feature-specific parameters such as `shelf2Z`, `hinge1Offset`, etc.

## Contract target

Reuse/evolve the existing versioned authoring envelope semantics from #346 rather than invent a parallel SketchUp-only model.

A request must carry enough context for deterministic stateless resolution, conceptually including:

```text
schema/version
request/message id
idempotency/correlation key where applicable
source catalog/definition revision
furniture definition identity
current authoring snapshot or reviewed semantic command + base snapshot
parameters/material choices
component occurrences
relationships/joint intent
manual hardware placements
units/coordinate-frame contract
```

Exact JSON/API shape must be reconciled with the existing #346 contract and current domain types before code.

## Mutation vs persistence boundary

This issue owns **authoring resolve/validation transport**, not Project business persistence.

Before #384/Gate A, the server may accept a stateless resolve command/snapshot and return the authoritative accepted/resolved result without creating a Project `FurnitureInstance` family.

After #384 connected mode exists, the same semantic contract should be reusable behind the appropriate Design working-copy command rather than replaced by a second API.

## Response target

Return a structured authoritative result that can drive atomic host rebuild and UX:

- accepted/rejected status;
- correlation IDs;
- version/schema/capability marker;
- normalized accepted authoring intent/snapshot or mutation receipt;
- authoritative native layout/scene data required by #415;
- exact stable occurrence identities;
- resolved hardware placements;
- structured preflight issues or a link/contract to obtain them;
- revision/fingerprint fields where the owning context already provides them;
- typed/stable error codes.

Do not make clients parse localized messages to decide behavior.

## Endpoint/transport rules

- use an explicit command/resolve endpoint; POST is appropriate even if operation is stateless/read-like because the payload is structured authoring intent;
- no state-changing semantics hidden behind GET query parameters;
- request payload limits and validation are explicit;
- unknown schema/version/capability fails safely;
- authorization requires explicit SketchUp capability appropriate to authoring resolution, not merely “GET means read-only/safe”;
- no bearer token in query string;
- logs sanitize authoring payload and credentials appropriately.

## Idempotency/concurrency

For stateless resolve, repeated identical requests must be deterministic and safe.

When the contract is later used against persisted Design working-copy state:

- consume version/ETag/baseRevision/idempotency conventions of the owning API;
- stale base cannot partially mutate server or host state;
- do not retrofit implicit `latest` semantics.

## Required canonical scenarios

1. Existing cabinet parameters/materials resolve identically to current layout endpoint semantics.
2. Move shelf → dependent relationships/machining update.
3. Add second shelf sharing reusable definition → distinct occurrence IDs/relationships.
4. Remove shelf → only dependent machining disappears.
5. Move manual hinge → hinge machining changes, shelf machining stable.
6. Replace hinge → hardware asset/BOM/machining resolve from selected compatible definition.
7. Invalid/orphan identity → structured rejection, no partial accepted result.
8. Unknown schema/version → fail closed.

