## Canonical pairing flow

A recommended flow is a short-lived one-time pairing grant:

```text
React user opens Project/Design
→ POST create pairing grant
→ backend authorizes active org + Project + Design + requested capability
→ return opaque one-time code / launch descriptor
→ Web attempts platform deep link and also shows copyable code
→ SketchUp exchanges code using its own client/session boundary
→ backend returns exact projectId/designId/baseRevisionId + capabilities
→ code becomes consumed/expired
→ plugin validates and writes model binding through #388
```

Exact deep-link scheme and transport are implementation choices. Security properties are mandatory.

## Security properties

The pairing grant must be:

- opaque and unguessable;
- one-time use;
- short TTL;
- scoped to exact organization, Project, Design and requested action;
- bound to the initiating user/session where appropriate;
- exchanged by the SketchUp client, never treated as a normal web access token;
- revocable/cancellable;
- rate limited;
- audited without logging the raw grant;
- unavailable to unrelated organizations even if the code is known.

No URL may contain:

- a reusable web JWT;
- SketchUp device/session credential;
- refresh credential;
- object-storage signed URL unrelated to the exact handoff.

## Web UX

Provide an accessible action in the exact Design context:

- `Abrir en SketchUp` when a supported installation/deep link is available;
- `Conectar con código` fallback;
- exact Project, Design and base revision shown before creation;
- expiration countdown/status without exposing secrets in analytics/logs;
- regenerate/cancel action;
- guidance when the plugin is missing or incompatible;
- clear handling for unauthorized, archived Design, stale revision, expired code and already-used code.

Do not report success merely because the browser invoked a custom URI. Success means the backend observed a valid exchange/binding acknowledgement or the UX clearly says the handoff was initiated but not confirmed.

## SketchUp UX

The plugin must:

- receive or accept the opaque code;
- exchange it through the dedicated SketchUp auth/client boundary;
- display exact organization/Project/Design/base revision before binding;
- detect an already-bound model and require an explicit reviewed switch/rebind flow;
- preserve the previous valid binding on exchange/validation failure;
- never infer Project/Design from filename, folder, persistent IDs or geometry;
- write model binding only after server validation succeeds;
- expose current connected state in the dialog.

## Rebind/switch policy

Switching a model from one Design to another is not a trivial metadata overwrite. The implementation must:

- inventory managed furniture identities in the current model;
- show consequences and blockers;
- prevent foreign/cross-project identities from silently becoming valid;
- route adoption/reconciliation needs to #397/#393;
- keep the old binding until the new binding is accepted.

## API and observability

Use #496 generated types and typed errors. At minimum correlate:

- pairing grant ID/hash reference;
- initiating web session/user/membership/org;
- exchanging SketchUp session/device;
- Project/Design/base revision;
- created/consumed/expired/revoked timestamps;
- request/trace IDs;
- sanitized outcome.

Raw codes and credentials are never logged or included in support bundles.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Web can initiate pairing for one exact authorized Project/Design/base revision.
- [ ] SketchUp exchanges the grant using a client-specific credential boundary, not the web JWT.
- [ ] Grant is one-time, short-lived, scoped, rate-limited and auditable.
- [ ] Cross-org, expired, revoked, replayed and already-used grants fail safely.
- [ ] Invoking a deep link is not represented as confirmed success by itself.
- [ ] Model binding is written only after authoritative validation.
- [ ] Existing valid binding survives any failed pairing/rebind.
- [ ] Bound context survives save/reopen through #388.
- [ ] Web and plugin show the same exact Project/Design/base revision.
- [ ] Unsupported plugin/API version blocks before metadata mutation.
- [ ] Browser E2E + backend integration + Ruby/TestUp cover happy path, missing plugin fallback, replay, cross-org, stale and rebind refusal.

## Negative proof required

Tests/review fail if:

- a web JWT appears in a custom URI/query string;
- a code can be exchanged twice;
- code possession alone bypasses organization/Design authorization;
- plugin writes binding before exchange validation;
- filename/path/UUID paste is the primary production workflow;
- rebind silently converts foreign FurnitureInstance IDs;
- Web announces “connected” only because `window.location` was changed.

## Definition of Done

A user can move from an exact Design in React to the matching connected context in SketchUp through a secure, observable and recoverable one-time handoff, with separate credentials, no URL token leakage, no inferred identity and no destructive rebind behavior.

