### Manufacturing artifact/job

- exact `ProductionRelease` and `designRevisionId`;
- exact BOM/manufacturing fingerprint;
- profile revision + adapter version/digest;
- status `queued|processing|ready|failed|blocked|expired` or canonical equivalent;
- structured issues/warnings;
- artifact MIME/size/SHA-256;
- authorized resource access metadata;
- creator/timestamps/audit references.

## Required React UX

### Machine Profiles

Factory-only administration:

- list/search/filter profiles;
- create/edit versioned profile through typed controls;
- capability matrix;
- clone/deprecate with explicit consequences;
- stale conflict/ETag handling;
- no brand-wide support implication from one profile.

### Evidence

Show per exact combination:

```text
machine + controller + software version
profile revision
adapter version/digest
result and limitations
```

The UI must clearly distinguish:

- validated;
- partial;
- unsupported;
- not tested.

Never render “compatible” merely because the brand/model name matches.

### Output generation

From an exact released production context:

1. select/confirm a permitted MachineProfile revision;
2. show compatibility/preflight result;
3. select exact adapter/output type;
4. request generation through an idempotent server command;
5. show queued/processing/failed/ready honestly;
6. download through resource-scoped authorization;
7. preserve immutable artifact manifest and checksum.

A retry must not create conflicting duplicate outputs for the same idempotency command.

### Release integration

#502 links to this workspace from an exact `ProductionRelease`. The Web must never generate from an arbitrary current Draft/Design without the owning policy explicitly allowing a non-production preview marked as such.

## Permissions and redaction

- Factory manufacturing/admin capabilities govern profiles and outputs.
- Store/partner projections receive only explicitly authorized commercial status, never machine internals or artifacts.
- Evidence packs contain no customer names, email, address, prices, credentials, hostnames or private paths.
- Artifact URLs contain no reusable session JWT.
- Support access follows scoped/audited #460/#461 behavior.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Factory user can inspect exact profile and adapter versions/capabilities.
- [ ] `not tested`, `partial`, `validated` and `unsupported` are distinct.
- [ ] One evidence pack cannot generalize support to another client/software version.
- [ ] Output generation uses exact ProductionRelease, fingerprint, profile revision and adapter digest.
- [ ] Unsupported capability blocks with structured remediation.
- [ ] Browser never produces machine format from geometry/domain data locally.
- [ ] Artifact status and failures are honest; no ready before server commit.
- [ ] Downloads use authorized resource-scoped access and integrity metadata.
- [ ] Retry/idempotency cannot create conflicting duplicate outputs.
- [ ] Cross-org and Store redaction are verified.
- [ ] React tests plus backend integration cover profile conflict, unsupported capability, processing failure, integrity and download expiry.

## Negative proof required

Tests/review fail if:

- UI infers compatibility from brand/name;
- PTX/CNC serialization appears in React or Ruby;
- artifact generation uses implicit latest Design or profile;
- Client A evidence marks Client B validated;
- failed generation is shown as ready;
- a reusable bearer JWT/private storage path appears in artifact URLs;
- Store users can access machine profile internals or manufacturing artifacts without an explicit grant.

## Definition of Done

The React application gives factories an exact, versioned and evidence-backed machine/output workflow tied to immutable ProductionRelease context, while all industrial resolution/postprocessing stays authoritative in Granete and all support claims remain limited to proven machine/software combinations.

## Objective

Provide Granete for SketchUp with privacy-safe, correlated diagnostics that a workshop user or support operator can export intentionally, tying host/runtime versions and sanitized request outcomes to backend audit/trace data without exposing credentials, private model content, customer data or local paths.

Tracks #355, #460, #461, #473 and #465.

## Why this is required

Commercial support cannot depend on asking users to open Ruby Console and manually copy arbitrary logs. At the same time, a generic log archive is unsafe because it may contain bearer tokens, emails, model paths, URLs, project data or proprietary geometry.

Granete already redacts several values and records host evidence, but it lacks a product workflow that produces a bounded, reviewable support artifact correlated with backend request/trace IDs.

## Mandatory authority

- `docs/architecture/sketchup-backend-web-integration-excellence.md` once merged
- `docs/verification.md`
- `docs/architecture/sketchup-plugin-excellence.md`
- #355 commercial release/support
- #460 sessions/media/security
- #461 audit/observability
- #473 compatibility matrix
- #496 generated error/correlation contract

## Hard prerequisites

- #496 request/error/capability correlation contract;
- critical SketchUp session model from #460;
- request/trace/audit foundation from #461 for end-to-end correlation.

A local sanitized environment summary may be implemented earlier, but the issue closes only when server-side correlation and privacy review are complete.

## Required diagnostic model

Create one bounded, versioned diagnostic manifest, conceptually:

```text
schemaVersion
createdAt
consentScope
pluginVersion
rbzSha256
sourceCommit/releaseChannel
osVersion/architecture
sketchupVersion/build
embeddedRubyVersion
cefVersion
apiBaseHostRedacted
apiContractVersion
supportedAuthoringSchemas
sessionClientType/status only
organization/project/design opaque IDs only when explicitly included
last N sanitized operations
requestId/traceId/messageId references
current compatibility/degraded state
model semantic inventory counts only
verification checks
redaction report
```

Exact fields require privacy review. The manifest must be closed/versioned and size bounded.

## Never include by default

- passwords;
- access/refresh/device tokens;
- cookies;
- authorization headers;
- pairing codes from #499;
- raw signed URLs;
- email/name/address/phone;
- customer/project commercial copy;
- BOM, dimensions, machining coordinates or proprietary model geometry;
- raw `.skp` file;
- full catalog payload;
- private absolute file paths, UNC shares or usernames from paths;
- arbitrary Ruby object dumps;
- unrestricted logs or browser storage.

Any future opt-in model/artifact attachment requires a separate explicit consent and policy, not a hidden checkbox.

## Required UX

Add a `Diagnóstico y soporte` surface in the plugin:

### Environment summary

Show user-readable:

- plugin/RBZ version;
- SketchUp/OS support classification from #473;
- API compatibility;
- session/license/degraded state;
- last connection/resolve/preflight outcome category;
- checks passed/failed;
- no raw tokens/UUID noise as primary UX.

### Create support bundle

Before export:

- explain exactly what will be included/excluded;
- allow user to preview the manifest in readable form;
- require explicit confirmation;
- generate locally into a chosen path without uploading automatically;
- show checksum and size;
- provide a separate reviewed upload/send flow only if/when backend support intake owns it.

### Correlation

Include only bounded recent references sufficient for backend operators to find:

- request ID;
- trace ID;
- message/correlation ID;
- sanitized timestamp/operation kind/result code.

Do not include full request bodies. Backend audit/access remains scoped and authorized.

## Redaction and deterministic verification

- Centralize redaction; do not rely on every caller remembering it.
- Add negative fixtures for bearer tokens, query credentials, POSIX/Windows/UNC paths, emails and sensitive context fields.
- Bundle generation performs a final secret/PII pattern scan and fails closed or removes unsafe fields.
- The manifest records which redaction checks ran, without echoing matched secrets.
- Generated bundle is deterministic enough for tests but timestamps/nonces may vary intentionally.

## Compatibility/support integration

- #473 evidence artifacts may link to a diagnostic schema/version, but a user bundle is not compatibility proof by itself.
- #355 release runbook documents how support reads the bundle and correlates it safely.
- Incompatible plugin/API state must still allow local diagnostic generation without mutating the model.
- Licensing/session failure cannot block the user from exporting local safe diagnostics.

## Backend/support intake boundary

If upload is implemented:

- dedicated endpoint/capability;
- short-lived upload authorization;
- content type/size/schema validation;
- malware/archive safety where applicable;
- retention/deletion policy;
- organization/support-session authorization;
- audit of upload/access/deletion;
- no generic media JWT query;
- user can see upload result and reference ID.

Automatic background upload is prohibited.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] User can view a readable local environment/support summary.
- [ ] Export requires explicit consent and preview.
- [ ] Bundle is versioned, bounded and contains only allowlisted fields.
- [ ] Tokens, cookies, pairing codes, signed URLs, emails and private paths are absent.
- [ ] No model geometry/BOM/machining/customer payload is included by default.
- [ ] Recent operations include only sanitized codes/timestamps/correlation references.
- [ ] Backend operator can correlate a request/trace ID under authorized scope.
- [ ] Secret/PII fixtures fail the final scan or are redacted.
- [ ] Bundle can be generated while offline/unauthenticated/incompatible.
- [ ] No automatic upload occurs.
- [ ] If upload exists, it is explicit, resource-scoped, audited and retention-controlled.
- [ ] Tests cover macOS/Windows path forms, query tokens, large/error logs, malformed manifest and consent cancellation.

## Negative proof required

Tests/review fail if:

- `Authorization`, cookie or session token appears anywhere in the archive;
- raw request/response authoring payload is dumped;
- private model path/user home path is present;
- a bundle uploads automatically on error/startup;
- support bundle is treated as proof that an unsupported host is supported;
- upload uses a generic session JWT in URL;
- user cannot inspect what will be shared.

## Definition of Done

Granete for SketchUp has a commercial-support diagnostic workflow that users can understand and control, operators can correlate with authorized backend telemetry, and automated privacy guards prove the bundle does not leak credentials, private paths, customer data or manufacturing/model content.

## Objective

Ensure Granete for SketchUp is not only technically correct but measurably easier, clearer and faster for its target furniture-factory designers than fragmented SketchUp workflows, through professional onboarding, excellent library discovery, contextual help and a repeatable usability benchmark based on real tasks.

Tracks #465 and #290. Coordinates with #466–#471, #499, #500, #504 and #355.

## Product principle

Granete must learn from universal market patterns visible in professional furniture tools—fast visual libraries, contextual properties, direct editing, repeat placement, batch operations and predictable feedback—without cloning proprietary UI or making unsupported competitive claims.

The differentiator to validate is not “more buttons”. It is continuity:

```text
find the right furniture
→ place/edit it confidently
→ understand/fix manufacturing blockers
→ connect the exact Project/Design
→ publish/review/release without identity or revision ambiguity
```

## Mandatory authority

- `docs/architecture/sketchup-backend-web-integration-excellence.md` once merged
- `docs/architecture/sketchup-plugin-excellence.md`
- `docs/architecture/sketchup-authoring-interaction-contract.md`
- `docs/design.md`
- `docs/verification.md`
- #498 shared host interaction/runtime
- #466–#471 professional workflow issues
- #499 secure Web↔SketchUp pairing

## Hard prerequisites

The final measured pilot requires the representative professional workflow:

- #466 preflight review/navigation;
- #467 internal component authoring;
- #468 HardwarePlacement editing/substitution;
- #469 placement/snapping/repeat;
- #470 manufacturing inspection overlay;
- #471 batch editing where included in the benchmark;
- #498 shared host runtime.

Onboarding/library discovery design and baseline observation may begin earlier. Project-connected benchmark scenarios wait for #388/#499/#500 and their Gate A prerequisites.

## Required scope

### First-run onboarding

Create a product-quality first-run experience that explains only the next useful steps:

1. connect/select the correct workshop/session;
2. understand local/unconnected vs connected Project mode;
3. open or pair an exact Project/Design when available;
4. find one furniture definition;
5. place it with preview/snap;
6. select furniture/part/hardware context;
7. edit one supported parameter/material;
8. run/review authoritative preflight;
9. know where diagnostics/help live.

Requirements:

- progressive, dismissible and restartable;
- does not block experienced users repeatedly;
- recognizes already-completed steps;
- no fake success before server/plugin confirmation;
- keyboard and screen-reader accessible where HtmlDialog supports it;
- copy in Spanish with technical IDs hidden from the primary flow;
- offline/license/incompatible states have honest alternate guidance.

### Library discovery

The furniture library must support professional discovery at realistic catalog sizes:

- search by name/code/category/approved tags;
- visual category hierarchy/breadcrumbs;
- useful thumbnails/previews and loading/error placeholders;
- filters based on authoritative definition capabilities/parameters;
- recent items;
- favorites or pinned items if product research proves value;
- compatible/available/incompatible/update-required states;
- keyboard navigation;
- repeat placement from recent/current item;
- no cross-organization catalog leakage;
- no local stale catalog presented as current.

Search/filter behavior must remain responsive at the measured catalog scale and may require virtualization/pagination; coordinate performance budgets with #472.

### Contextual guidance

Provide concise help where users get blocked:

- why an action is unavailable from capability/selection state;
- difference between manual and derived hardware;
- meaning of stale/offline/unresolved;
- remediation from preflight;
- exact next action rather than generic documentation links;
- shortcut/help reference for select, place, rotate, precise mm input, undo and inspection.

Do not expose manufacturing internals or schema terminology unnecessarily.

### Recovery and empty states

Test and design distinct experiences for:

- no session;
- no active license;
- no catalog items;
- search with no results;
- catalog loading/error;
- unsupported plugin/API version;
- unbound model;
- Project with no physical furniture;
- stale Design base;
- offline with previously resolved furniture;
- legacy migration required;
- no managed selection.

An empty/error state must explain the next safe action and never create generic productive fallback.

## Measured usability benchmark

### Canonical tasks

Use a disposable canonical workshop/project and measure at least:

1. install/open plugin and identify current workshop/status;
2. find and place a base cabinet without origin-first workaround;
3. change a BODY material and one typed parameter;
4. select/move/add a shelf;
5. select/move or replace a hinge;
6. create, understand, navigate and fix a real preflight blocker;
7. inspect one manufacturing feature/provenance;
8. apply one safe multi-selection operation when #471 is included;
9. pair/open the exact Project/Design from React when #499 is available;
10. recover from one network/stale/error scenario without corrupting the model.

### Metrics

Record actual, contextual measurements rather than invented targets:

- task completion rate;
- time on task;
- wrong turns/backtracks;
- destructive or identity-risk errors;
- requests for help;
- number of modal/context switches;
- confidence/ease rating with stated methodology;
- accessibility/keyboard completion where applicable;
- first-use vs repeat-use difference;
- host/hardware/catalog fixture context.

After baseline measurement, approve budgets/targets and record them in the issue/verification artifact. Do not choose attractive numbers before measuring.

### Participants/personas

Include representative target workflows, for example:

- workshop designer/engineer familiar with SketchUp;
- sales/design user with furniture knowledge but less CAD depth;
- administrator/catalog author for the React→SketchUp definition flow where applicable.

Use actual consenting participants available to the project. Do not claim statistical generalization from a tiny pilot. Record limitations and no personal data in artifacts.

### Competitive reference method

Where accessible and lawful, observe equivalent public/product workflows in representative market tools or documented demos. Compare task structure and user friction, not proprietary code, assets, exact layouts or unsupported internal assumptions.

All conclusions are labeled:

```text
observed
measured in Granete
inferred
not tested
```

Do not publish “faster than X” without comparable measured evidence and permission/context appropriate for the claim.

## Integration feedback loop

Every benchmark finding is classified:

- blocker before pilot/release;
- P1 product improvement;
- P2 polish;
- training/documentation issue;
- unsupported/out-of-scope.

Findings update the owning issue rather than being hidden in a report. Examples:

- selection confusion → #476/#466;
- mutation friction → #467/#468/#498;
- placement friction → #469;
- catalog discovery → this issue/#497;
- performance → #472;
- pairing confusion → #499;
- diagnostic/recovery confusion → #474/#504.

## Accessibility and localization

- keyboard access for primary dialog tasks;
- visible focus and logical order;
- meaningful labels/aria-live for loading/error/resolve state;
- no color-only severity/status;
- text resilient to scaling/long Spanish labels;
- WCAG AA contrast for HtmlDialog UI where technically applicable;
- translation-ready copy structure even if Spanish is the first commercial language.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] First-run onboarding is progressive, restartable, state-aware and does not repeatedly block experienced users.
- [ ] A new user can reach a correctly placed managed cabinet through the intended preview/snap flow.
- [ ] Library search/category/filter states remain understandable and tenant-safe.
- [ ] Unavailable actions explain the capability/selection reason and next safe action.
- [ ] Empty, no-results, error, offline, stale, incompatible and legacy states are distinct.
- [ ] Canonical tasks are run with representative consenting users and actual measurements.
- [ ] Baseline, approved targets and limitations are documented without invented metrics.
- [ ] At least one repeat-use run demonstrates whether onboarding/help becomes unnecessary rather than permanently intrusive.
- [ ] Findings are mapped to owner issues with severity and evidence.
- [ ] Keyboard/a11y tests cover primary dialog flows.
- [ ] No competitive superiority claim is made without comparable evidence.
- [ ] Real-host evidence records SketchUp/OS/RBZ/catalog fixture and preserves privacy.

## Negative proof required

Tests/review fail if:

- onboarding reports connected/inserted/saved before authoritative confirmation;
- tutorial creates generic fallback furniture as if productive;
- library search leaks another organization's catalog or treats stale cache as current;
- unavailable actions have no explanation/remediation;
- benchmark records only subjective opinions with no task evidence;
- targets are invented before baseline measurement;
- one expert user is presented as universal market validation;
- UI copies a competitor's proprietary visual design/assets;
- accessibility is represented only by source grep or color inspection;
- usability findings are documented but never linked to owning backlog work.

## Definition of Done

Granete for SketchUp has a professional first-run and daily-use experience, a fast and understandable library, contextual recovery/help and a repeatable real-host usability benchmark proving that target users can complete the core authoring→preflight→Project workflow confidently, with measured evidence driving final product fixes before commercial release.

