issues.body:
## Objective

Add **interactive opening/closing kinematics to `Agregado`** so Granete can visually open doors, drawers and lift fronts in both **Proyectar 3D (React/R3F)** and **Granete for SketchUp**, producing a high-impact presentation/demo interaction without changing BOM, manufacturing geometry or DesignRevision truth.

Tracks #308 (Proyectar 3D North Star) and #465/#290 (Granete for SketchUp excellence).

## Product decision — `Agregado` is the movable unit

The current domain already defines `Agregado` as a reusable sub-assembly composed of component instances + hardware, explicitly including examples such as **drawers** and **doors with hinges/handle**. Keep motion in that layer instead of adding parallel motion semantics to `Component`.

Canonical rule:

```text
Component = reusable physical part/component definition
Agregado  = reusable semantic sub-assembly that may move as one presentation unit
```

Therefore:

- a swing door should be an `Agregado`, even when it contains only one front component + hinges/handle;
- a drawer is one `Agregado` containing front + box parts + slides/handle;
- a movable lift/front should be an `Agregado`;
- a fixed decorative/front board that never moves may remain a normal component/board;
- do **not** add `openDirection`, `hingeSide`, etc. independently to `Component` in this slice.

This intentionally reduces the number of places that own opening semantics.

## Architectural invariant — presentation pose is NOT authoring/manufacturing truth

Opening/closing is a **presentation/interaction pose** over the accepted closed geometry.

```text
accepted closed geometry
+ transient opening progress
= rendered pose
```

Opening a door/drawer MUST NOT:

- change BOM;
- change dimensions/material choices;
- change machining;
- change `bomFingerprint`;
- mark a Design/ProductionRelease stale;
- create a new DesignRevision;
- submit `MoveComponentIntent`;
- persist raw moved coordinates as manufacturing truth.

`progress=0` is always the canonical closed pose. Every rendered pose is recomputed from the immutable/base closed transform; never accumulate incremental transforms.

## Domain model

Add one optional presentation-motion definition to `Agregado` (exact naming may follow conventions), conceptually:

```ts
type AgregadoPresentationMotion =
  | RotateMotion
  | TranslateMotion
  | KeyframedMotion;

interface Agregado {
  // existing fields...
  readonly presentationMotion?: AgregadoPresentationMotion;
}
```

### 1. Rotate motion

Covers most doors/fronts:

```ts
{
  kind: 'rotate',
  pivot: 'left' | 'right' | 'top' | 'bottom' | customLocalPivot,
  axis: localAxis,
  openAngleDeg: number
}
```

Required use cases:

- side-hinged door left;
- side-hinged door right;
- upward flap / basculante;
- downward flap;
- generic top-pivot lift front;
- AVENTOS HK-like visual motion can use top rotation in V1.

Renderer adapters must consume resolved numeric local pivot/axis, not maintain furniture-name/role heuristics.

### 2. Translate motion

Covers drawers and sliding fronts:

```ts
{
  kind: 'translate',
  axis: localAxis,
  distanceMm | distanceFormula
}
```

Required V1 use case:

- drawer translating out of the furniture along its local depth/front axis.

The whole drawer Agregado moves, not only its front.

### 3. Keyframed transform motion

Provide an extensibility path for lift systems whose visible path is not a simple hinge or straight slide:

```ts
{
  kind: 'keyframes',
  keyframes: [
    { progress: 0, translationMm: [...], rotation: ... },
    ...,
    { progress: 1, translationMm: [...], rotation: ... }
  ]
}
```

Use generic mechanism profiles/presets rather than hardcoding vendor names into rendering logic.

This enables visual equivalents of:

- parallel lift (AVENTOS HL-like);
- up-and-over lift (AVENTOS HS-like);
- other future special paths.

A bi-fold lift (AVENTOS HF-like) requires multiple linked actors/panels and may be delivered as the final sub-slice of this issue after the single-actor MVP. The contract must not prevent it.

## Generic motion vocabulary, not brand-specific physics

V1 is a **visual/presentation kinematic model**, not a mechanical simulation of hinge arms, springs or lift-force curves.

Suggested generic presets/UI concepts:

```text
swing_left
swing_right
flap_up
flap_down
drawer_pull
parallel_lift
up_and_over
bi_fold_lift (multi-actor extension)
```

Hardware catalog/domain may later map a specific mechanism to one of these motion profiles, but Web/Ruby must not contain `if hardware == BLUM_...` logic.

## Resolved motion contract

The renderer should not infer moving parts by names, `FRONT`, geometry or array order.

Resolution should provide a stable result equivalent to:

```ts
interface ResolvedAgregadoMotion {
  readonly agregadoInstanceId: string;
  readonly motion: ResolvedPresentationMotion;
  readonly componentInstanceIds: readonly string[];
}
```

Where required for SketchUp hardware siblings, the resolved/presentation adapter must also be able to associate hardware placements with the same motion through `hostComponentInstanceId` / placement identity.

Rules:

- one Agregado instance has independent presentation state from another copy;
- repeated/stacked drawers each get distinct resolved motion identity;
- component/hardware membership comes from authoritative aggregate resolution, never display names;
- rotated/moved top-level furniture still opens correctly because motion is in furniture-local coordinates.

## React / R3F — Proyectar 3D

Current renderer: `packages/ui/src/preview3d/FurnitureScene3D.tsx`.

Implement a presentation-pose adapter around existing board/hardware rendering.

Required behavior:

- state key equivalent to `moduleInstanceKey + agregadoInstanceId`;
- `progress` normalized `0..1`;
- smooth interpolation using R3F frame loop/easing without physics dependency;
- recompute from closed/base transform every frame;
- hardware attached to moving board/aggregate follows it;
- multiple drawers can be independently opened;
- multiple copies of the same furniture definition keep independent state;
- top-level cabinet rotation/placement does not break local opening axis.

### UX

Minimum:

- contextual `Abrir / Cerrar` action for a movable Agregado;
- direct interaction in presentation mode where it does not conflict with editing/selection;
- `Abrir todos` / `Cerrar todos` for the selected furniture or presentation scene if cheap to add;
- visual hover/cursor feedback only when the target is actually movable.

Do not sacrifice normal selection/drag semantics in Proyectar. If direct single-click conflicts with editing, use inspector action/double-click/presentation-mode interaction rather than fragile event priority hacks.

## SketchUp adapter

Use the existing native `ComponentInstance` hierarchy and authoritative local transforms from #414/#415.

Required behavior:

- apply the presentation motion from each child's canonical closed transform;
- use furniture-local axes/pivots, never world-axis assumptions;
- closing returns exactly to the canonical resolved transform with no drift;
- drawer motion moves all component instances belonging to the drawer Agregado;
- hardware belonging to moving hosts follows the same presentation pose even if native SketchUp hierarchy renders hardware as sibling instances;
- opening/closing must not emit authoring intents or become manufacturing truth;
- a later authoritative rebuild/re-resolve resets/rebases the presentation pose safely;
- save/reopen/publish paths must never interpret an open visual pose as a changed productive layout.

### SketchUp UX minimum

- selected managed furniture/child can expose `Abrir / Cerrar` when it belongs to a movable Agregado;
- furniture inspector may list its openings if this is simpler/safer for the first host slice;
- `Cerrar todos` must be available before/around publish/rebuild if needed to guarantee closed host representation;
- tests prove no confusion with #467 `MoveComponentIntent` or #468 hardware authoring.

## MVP motion matrix

| Use case | V1 mechanism | Required |
|---|---|---:|
| Door left | rotate around left vertical pivot | ✅ |
| Door right | rotate around right vertical pivot | ✅ |
| Drawer | translate whole Agregado outward | ✅ |
| Flap up | rotate around top horizontal pivot | ✅ |
| Flap down | rotate around bottom horizontal pivot | ✅ |
| HK-like lift | top rotate preset | ✅ |
| HL-like parallel lift | keyframed transform | target in same issue |
| HS-like up-and-over | keyframed transform | target in same issue |
| HF-like bi-fold | multi-actor linked keyframes | last sub-slice / may be deferred without blocking MVP if explicitly documented |

## Fast delivery strategy

Keep this as one feature issue but implement in vertical slices so the demo-visible value lands before exotic mechanisms:

### Slice A — contract + Web MVP

```text
Agregado.presentationMotion
→ resolved actor membership
→ R3F rotate/translate
→ side door + flap + drawer
```

### Slice B — SketchUp parity

```text
same resolved motion
→ native ComponentInstance presentation transforms
→ door + drawer + flap
```

### Slice C — special lift paths

```text
keyframes
→ parallel lift / up-and-over
→ optional linked bi-fold actor support
```

Do not block A/B on perfect vendor-specific lift geometry.

## Canonical demo fixture

Create one small demo set containing:

1. base cabinet with left/right swing doors;
2. 3-drawer cabinet — each drawer independently movable;
3. upper cabinet with `flap_up`;
4. upper cabinet with `flap_down` or bar/front flap where appropriate;
5. upper cabinet with one keyframed lift profile.

Demo interaction:

```text
open project
→ click/open cabinet door
→ reveal interior
→ open individual drawers
→ open upper lift front
→ close all
```

The exact same semantic fixture should be usable in Web and SketchUp tests where practical.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Motion semantics live on `Agregado`, not duplicated on `Component`.
- [ ] A movable single front can be represented as an Agregado of one front + hardware.
- [ ] A drawer moves as a complete sub-assembly, not just its front.
- [ ] Side doors open left/right using local pivots.
- [ ] Front can rotate upward and downward.
- [ ] At least one generic lift profile can use keyframed rotation+translation.
- [ ] Two identical furniture instances can have different opening states.
- [ ] Three drawers can be independently open at different progress values.
- [ ] Rotating the whole cabinet does not change the semantic opening direction.
- [ ] Hardware/handles on moving hosts follow the moving assembly.
- [ ] Open → close repeated many times produces no transform drift.
- [ ] Web and SketchUp consume the same semantic/resolved motion contract.
- [ ] Opening/closing leaves BOM, machining, fingerprint, DesignRevision and ProductionRelease state unchanged.
- [ ] Authoritative rebuild/re-resolve returns to a known closed/base geometry without losing identity.
- [ ] Tests cover rotate, translate, top/bottom pivot, independent instances and negative manufacturing-authority proof.

## Negative proof required

Tests/review fail if:

- renderer decides motion from names such as `PUERTA`/`CAJON`/`FRONT`;
- Web and SketchUp define separate incompatible opening models;
- opening writes raw moved coordinates back as productive authoring intent;
- opening a door marks design/manufacturing stale;
- only drawer front moves while drawer box remains closed;
- a rotated cabinet still opens along a global/world axis;
- closing is performed by applying an inverse delta to the current pose instead of recomputing from canonical closed transform;
- one copy's opening state affects another copy;
- hardware remains floating at the closed pose while its host front moves;
- vendor-specific names are hardcoded in renderer/domain branching.

## Explicit non-goals

- mechanical/physics simulation of springs, gas pistons or force curves;
- collision solving between multiple open fronts in V1;
- exact branded kinematic engineering of every AVENTOS generation;
- changing hinge/machining definitions when merely previewing an opening;
- using open pose as manufacturing/export geometry;
- CAD/free transform authoring.

## Definition of Done

A user can interactively open/close common movable furniture sub-assemblies in Proyectar 3D and Granete for SketchUp — side doors, drawers and up/down lift fronts — from one `Agregado`-owned semantic motion model. The interaction is smooth, local-coordinate correct and demo-ready while remaining completely isolated from manufacturing/design truth, with the contract extensible to parallel/up-and-over/bi-fold lift mechanisms.

